(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-ent-antihistamines-steroids-quiz"]={"title":"Antihistamines & Corticosteroids Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which agent is named as an over-the-counter sleep aid?","choices":["Azelastine","Promethazine","Doxylamine","Fluticasone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sedating antihistamine sold for the side effect rather than the indication. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is a patient on prednisone told about infection risk?","choices":["They should take prophylactic antibiotics","Infection risk only matters above a certain amount","They are immunocompromised and should avoid chickenpox","There is no increased risk with short courses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Live vaccines are the part most easily missed, because the patient does not think of a vaccine as an exposure. Measles and live vaccines. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Beyond histamine receptors, what else do first generation agents act on?","choices":["Adrenergic receptors only","Muscarinic and serotonergic systems","Dopamine receptors in the basal ganglia","Nothing else -- they are entirely selective"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acting on several receptor families is what gives them uses and side effects that have nothing to do with allergy. With antiemetic action. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the major central nervous system side effect of first generation H1 antagonists?","choices":["Tremor","Headache","Seizures at ordinary use","Sedation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the dominant side effect, and it is also the basis of an entire over-the-counter market in sleep aids. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which eye findings are associated with systemic corticosteroids?","choices":["High intraocular pressure, glaucoma and cataract","Retinal detachment and vitreous hemorrhage","Optic neuritis","Corneal ulceration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Worth holding onto because a course given for the nose can end up costing sight if it runs long enough. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A patient on a diuretic is started on dexamethasone. What should be monitored?","choices":["Liver enzymes and bilirubin","Thyroid function tests","Potassium, since both drugs lower it","Prothrombin time and clotting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two drugs pushing the same electrolyte the same way, which is how a patient arrives at a dangerous level without either drug being at fault alone. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is azelastine nasal spray indicated for?","choices":["Bacterial sinusitis","Allergic and vasomotor rhinitis","Nasal congestion from the common cold","Epistaxis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It covers vasomotor rhinitis as well as allergic, which matters because vasomotor rhinitis has no allergic trigger to avoid. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why do second generation antihistamines cause much less sedation?","choices":["They do not enter the central nervous system","They block H2 rather than H1 receptors","They are given at much lower exposure","They are broken down before reaching the brain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The whole generational difference comes down to where the drug goes rather than what it blocks. Not to the same extent, at any rate, which is the whole generational difference. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A child given a first generation antihistamine becomes restless and agitated rather than sleepy. How is that explained?","choices":["The child has developed tolerance to the sedating effect","The drug was a second generation agent","It indicates an allergic reaction to the antihistamine","Excitation occurs at higher exposure, and particularly in children"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The paradox is recognized rather than unusual, and it means an agitated child may have had more of the drug rather than less. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which effect belongs to H2 rather than H1 receptors?","choices":["Increased gastric acid secretion","Stimulation of sensory nerve endings","Increased post-capillary permeability","Bronchoconstriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the reason H2 blockers are stomach drugs while H1 blockers are allergy drugs. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How do antihistamines reduce nasal and bronchial secretions during an allergic reaction?","choices":["By blocking H2 receptors on glands","By constricting the vessels supplying the mucosa","By thinning the mucus already produced","Through their antimuscarinic action"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The drying is a muscarinic effect rather than a histamine one, which is why the second generation agents dry you out less. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why is a patient told to use the right hand for the left nostril and the left hand for the right?","choices":["It distributes the medicine more evenly","It angles the spray away from the septum","It prevents contamination of the bottle tip","It reduces the bitter taste"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Crossing hands sets the angle automatically, and the septum is what nosebleed and perforation happen to. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Histamine release produces the triple response. What are its three components?","choices":["Redness, itching and sneezing","Redness, a wheal, and a surrounding flare","Swelling, pallor and numbness","Bronchospasm, hypotension and rash"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three visible signs from three different mechanisms -- direct vasodilation, leaking capillaries, and a nerve reflex -- which is why they appear together but look different. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What side effects are described for azelastine nasal spray?","choices":["A bitter taste and nosebleed","Sedation and dry mouth","Rebound congestion","Raised intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The bitter taste is the common reason patients stop it, and it comes from spray running back into the throat. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"H1 and H2 receptors both cause vasodilation. How do the two differ?","choices":["H1 is rapid in onset and short-lived","H1 is slow and sustained, H2 is rapid and brief","Both are rapid and brief","Only H1 causes vasodilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Same end result by two different routes and on two different timescales, which is why blocking H1 alone does not abolish the flush. H2 is slow in onset and long-lasting. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A patient with diabetes is started on a course of prednisone. What should be anticipated?","choices":["Glucose will fall and treatment may need reducing","Glucose control will worsen","There is no interaction with antidiabetic medicines","Insulin requirements will fall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two effects pointing the same way: the steroid raises glucose and blunts the drugs used to lower it. Antidiabetic medicines will work less well. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which adverse effects are specifically associated with topical use of antihistamines?","choices":["Sedation, drowsiness and confusion","Urinary retention and dry mouth","Dermatitis and photosensitivity of the skin","Epistaxis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Applying an antihistamine to skin can produce the kind of reaction it was meant to treat. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why is a patient who has taken a corticosteroid for more than about a week told not to stop abruptly?","choices":["The drug accumulates and must be cleared slowly","Abrupt withdrawal causes fluid retention","The drug loses effect if restarted after a gap","Stopping suddenly produces rebound symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the duration that creates the problem, which is why a short course and a long one are stopped differently. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 46"}]};
